  doi = {10.1109/LSP.2018.2843524},
  abstract = {Correlation-filter-based trackers, showing strong discrimination ability to challenging situations, have recently achieved superior performance in visual tracking. However, for the model treats the tracker's predictions in new frames as training data, the filter can be contaminated by small incorrect predictions which cause model drift. Particle-filter-based trackers usually produce more accurate results due to the richer image representations used to predict, but suffer when the environments are complex throughout an image sequence. In this letter, we take an innovative real-time algorithm, by putting more emphasis on combining the particle filter with correlation filters in the prediction stage, where accurate predictions inferred by the particle filter alleviate model drift. Moreover, an effective decision fusion strategy is proposed to get more precise object predictions, thus further enhancing the overall tracking performance. Extensive evaluations on the OTB-2013 benchmark demonstrate that the proposed tracker is very promising compared with the state-of-the-art trackers while operating faster than 85 frames per second.},
